steps.body:
Depth-first walks go deep first. Level-order traversal visits the tree one row at a time, top to bottom and left to right. It is the level-by-level BFS from the BFS lesson, with a `std::queue` of node pointers in place of grid cells.

```cpp
std::queue<const TreeNode*> q;
q.push(root);
while (!q.empty()) {
    const std::size_t levelSize = q.size();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
        const TreeNode* node = q.front();
        q.pop();
        // print node->value, then push its children
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}
```

When the outer loop starts a round, the queue holds exactly one level, so saving `q.size()` first processes just that level while the children wait for the next round. A tree needs no visited marker: every node has exactly one parent, so it is pushed exactly once.

Push only children that exist: `if (node->left != nullptr)`. A null pointer in the queue would be dereferenced on a later round. Check the root too, because an empty tree has nothing to push. The queue copies pointers, not nodes, so the tree is untouched and still needs `freeTree`, the postorder delete, at the end.

